But direct read of the actual code shows:
- Step 12 sets approval_status to the hardcoded literal 'Pending' — always, unconditionally, no branching on the elevation reasoning at all.
- Step 18 selects the subject line via: `inp.approval_status === 'Pending-Elevated' ? '[ELEVATED...]' : '[APPROVAL REQUIRED]...'`

Since the new 4-value scheme has no 'Pending-Elevated' value at all, and Step 12 never produces it, this check in Step 18 appears to be permanently dead — every approval email would get the generic subject line regardless of tier, governance flag, or commercial-commitment detection, even though elevation_reason itself does correctly appear with real content in the email body. This directly contradicts v5.0's claim that the mechanism "still drives" the subject line.

Unlike the Language Router wiring (already confirmed correct against the live system), THIS discrepancy has NOT yet been verified either way. Before finalizing v5.1, confirm directly against the live n8n editor: does Step 18 currently produce two visually distinct subject lines depending on elevation status, or does every approval email currently look identical regardless of severity? If the latter, the fix is small: either have Step 12 set a real elevated-status marker when reasons.length > 0, or have Step 18 check elevation_reason (or human_review_required, or confirmed_response_tier === 'T3') directly instead of a status string value the new schema no longer produces.

